  "live_records": {
    "q-329001": {
      "dom_id": "step-q329001",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}].$ \n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of $A$ is $3,$ and it has multiplicity two.*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" height=\"30px\" align=\"center\">${e}^{At}={e}^{3t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td width=\"5px\" height=\"40px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"80px\" align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td width=\"80px\" align=\"center\">$2t$</td>\n<td width=\"5px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td width=\"30px\" align=\"center\">$2t+1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr></tbody></table>",
      "worked_solution": "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & 2{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ -2{\\alpha }_{1}t & 5{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The only eigenvalue or $At$ is \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=3t\n$$\n\nof multiplicity two. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}r(3t)={e}^{3t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}\\lambda }r(3t)={e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}3t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{3t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{3t}.\n$$\n\n- Substituting this into the first one, we get\n\n$$\n3t{e}^{3t}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{3t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{3t}(1-3t).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & ={e}^{3t}t+{e}^{3t}(1-3t) \\\\  & ={e}^{3t}(1-2t) \\\\ -2{\\alpha }_{1}t & =-2{e}^{3t}t \\\\  & ={e}^{3t}(-2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}={e}^{3t}[\\begin{aligned}1-2t & 2t \\\\ -2t & 2t+1\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
