    "q-334631": {
      "dom_id": "step-q334631",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -10 \\\\ 5 & 7\\end{aligned}].$ \n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $2\\pm 5\\text{i}.$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" height=\"30px\" align=\"center\">${e}^{At}={e}^{2t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td width=\"5px\" height=\"40px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n<td width=\"140px\" align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td width=\"140px\" align=\"center\">$-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)$</td>\n<td width=\"5px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td width=\"30px\" align=\"center\">$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr></tbody></table>",
      "worked_solution": "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -10 \\\\ 5 & 7\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & -10{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 5{\\alpha }_{1}t & 7{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues or $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=(2+5\\text{i})t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=(2-5\\text{i})t,\n$$\n\nwhich are complex conjugates. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r((2+5\\text{i})t) & ={e}^{(2+5\\text{i})t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{1}(2+5\\text{i})t+{\\alpha }_{0} & ={e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)) \\\\ (2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0})+5{\\alpha }_{1}\\text{i}t & ={e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)+\\text{i}\\,{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we equate the real and imaginary parts, and solve for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\ 5t{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{1}=\\frac{1}{5t}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t))\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{5t}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\cdot t+{e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-\\frac{2}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{5}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-\\frac{2}{5}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\  & ={e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)) \\\\ 5{\\alpha }_{1}t & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{5t}{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\cdot t \\\\  & ={e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}={e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) & -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
